## Storage backends

Switch persistence via `storage.backend` in `config.yaml` (or `STORAGE__BACKEND` env):

| Backend | When to use | Notes |
|---------|-------------|-------|
| `jsonl` *(default)* | Quick experimentation, portability | Writes `data/<target>.jsonl` |
| `sql` (SQLite) | Local single-file DB | `sqlite+aiosqlite:///./data/scraper.db` |
| `sql` (PostgreSQL) | Production, shared DB | `postgresql+asyncpg://user:pass@host:5432/scraper` |
| `sql` (Supabase) | Hosted Postgres + auth | `postgresql+asyncpg://postgres:<pwd>@db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432/postgres` |
| `mongo` | *(planned)* | NotImplemented |
| `kafka` | *(planned)* | NotImplemented |

Bootstrap the SQL schema once:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} db init       # create_all()
{{ project_slug }} db drop -y    # drop_all() (destructive)
```

The same `ScrapedItem` shape is written to every backend, so you can switch
without touching scraper code.

## Record shape & joins

Every persisted record (JSONL or SQL) has this canonical shape:

```jsonc
{
  "job_id":      "<uuid>",      // shared by every page in one scrape run
  "record_id":   "<uuid>",      // unique per page; 1:1 with this row
  "scraper_key": "httpx",       // "httpx" | "playwright" | <your_scraper>
  "target":      "example",     // config.yaml target name
  "url":         "https://...",
  "status":      200,
  "scraped_at":  "2026-05-14T...",
  "data":        { /* extracted business data */ },
  "metadata":    {
    "stats":     { /* per-page counters + timings (see below) */ },
    "artifacts": {
      "count": 3, "total_bytes": 184502,
      "by_kind": { "html": "...", "markdown": "...", "screenshot": "..." },
      "items": [
        {"kind": "html",       "path": "...", "media_type": "text/html",
         "size_bytes": 12345, "created_at": "..."},
        {"kind": "markdown",   "path": "...", "media_type": "text/markdown",
         "size_bytes": 2100,  "created_at": "..."},
        {"kind": "screenshot", "path": "...", "media_type": "image/png",
         "size_bytes": 170057, "width": 1366, "height": 768,
         "extra": {"full_page": true}, "created_at": "..."}
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

`job_id` is the canonical join key for downstream analytics and job
monitoring. Each invocation of `{{ project_slug }} run` (or `--job-id` for
explicit reuse) gets a fresh `uuid4`, propagated to every page it produces.
In SQL the column is indexed.

## Page artifacts

Files produced during a page scrape (raw HTML, Markdown rendering,
screenshots, video recordings, ...) are modelled as first-class
`PageArtifact` records:

```python
@dataclass
class PageArtifact:
    kind: str             # "html" | "markdown" | "screenshot" | "video" | ...
    path: str             # absolute / project-relative filesystem path
    media_type: str       # MIME type (auto-detected from extension + kind)
    size_bytes: int | None
    width: int | None     # screenshots / videos
    height: int | None
    duration_ms: float | None  # videos
    extra: dict[str, Any] # arbitrary side-channel info (codec, checksum, ...)
    created_at: datetime
```

Toggles (see `config.yaml`):

| Setting | What it produces |
|---------|------------------|
| `scraper.save_raw_html` | One `.html` file per fetched page |
| `scraper.save_markdown` | One `.md` file per page (HTML → markdown via `markdownify`) |
| `playwright.screenshot` | One `.png`/`.jpeg` per page |
| `playwright.record_video` | One `.webm` per page navigation |

Where artifacts land:

* **Inline** — every artifact is serialised under `metadata.artifacts` on
  the JSONL / SQL record (see shape above).
* **First-class SQL table** — `page_artifacts` rows with indexed columns
  for `record_id`, `job_id`, `target`, and `kind`. Query "all videos
  recorded in run `<job_id>`" with a single indexed lookup:

  ```sql
  SELECT path, size_bytes, duration_ms
  FROM page_artifacts
  WHERE job_id = '...' AND kind = 'video';
  ```

To register a custom artifact from your own scraper:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.artifacts import record_artifact

await page.screenshot(path=str(shot_path))
record_artifact("screenshot", shot_path, width=1366, height=768)
```

## Page stats

Every page-scrape produces a `PageStats` instance covering identifiers,
counters, timing, and a quality heuristic. The full schema lives under
`metadata.stats` on each record:

| Field | What it tracks |
|-------|----------------|
| `job_id` / `record_id` / `scraper_key` | Joinable IDs duplicated for analytics |
| `page_requests` | HTTP / Playwright navigations |
| `clicks` | Explicit page interactions (Playwright) |
| `screenshots` | Files written by `page.screenshot(...)` |
| `videos` | Per-page `.webm` recordings (when `playwright.record_video=true`) |
| `llm_calls` / `llm_input_tokens` / `llm_output_tokens` | LLM usage (call `record_llm_call(in, out)` from your wrapper) |
| `extractions_succeeded` / `extractions_failed` | Outcome of `parse_*` calls |
| `extraction_time_ms` | Cumulative time spent inside `extracting()` blocks |
| `extraction_started_at` / `extraction_finished_at` | First-extraction-start / last-extraction-end timestamps |
| `start_timestamp` / `end_timestamp` / `duration_ms` | Wall-clock for the whole page scope |
| `page_complexity` | 0-100 heuristic from DOM nodes, scripts, iframes, forms, bytes |

Use it from your own scraper code:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.stats import (
    extracting, page_stats, record_llm_call, record_request, set_complexity,
)

async def scrape_one(url: str, job_id: str) -> dict:
    with page_stats(
        target="my_target", url=url, job_id=job_id, scraper_key="my_scraper",
    ) as stats:
        resp = await fetch_url(url)
        record_request()
        set_complexity(html=resp.text)

        record_llm_call(input_tokens=120, output_tokens=60)

        with extracting():            # times + records success/failure
            data = parse(resp.text)

        return {
            "job_id": stats.job_id,
            "record_id": stats.record_id,
            "scraper_key": stats.scraper_key,
            "url": url,
            "status": resp.status_code,
            "data": data,
            "metadata": {"stats": stats.to_dict()},
        }
```

The httpx + Playwright runners and `{{ project_slug }} run` already wire these
helpers automatically. The CLI prints a session-totals table (including
`job id`) at the end of every run, and each record's per-page stats land
under `metadata.stats` in the chosen backend.

